WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:03.180
All right, before we hop into the engine,

2
00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.379
we're gonna cover the animation pipelines

3
00:00:05.379 --> 00:00:09.339
from traditional to more of the modern with Unreal Engine.

4
00:00:09.339 --> 00:00:12.259
So normally you would start in your separate DCC program

5
00:00:12.259 --> 00:00:14.339
and you'd model, rig, and animate.

6
00:00:14.339 --> 00:00:15.380
And then once you were finished,

7
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:16.859
you'd import that finished animation

8
00:00:16.859 --> 00:00:18.859
directly into Unreal Engine.

9
00:00:18.859 --> 00:00:21.460
Now you could take it further into Sequencer,

10
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:24.219
edit things, add some more tracks to it

11
00:00:24.219 --> 00:00:27.000
from whatever you would want for other reason.

12
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.179
Now, you could also now edit in Sequencer,

13
00:00:30.219 --> 00:00:32.579
the option you would edit with the FK control rig,

14
00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:35.420
or even bake it to an existing control rig

15
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.140
and work on that even further.

16
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.479
Now here's a typical animation studio pipeline.

17
00:00:40.479 --> 00:00:42.740
As you can see, this is very traditional.

18
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:44.880
You've got everything set up in the different departments

19
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.420
at work alongside, kind of in parallel,

20
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.929
and the very typical setup.

21
00:00:50.929 --> 00:00:53.710
Now the newer in-editor animation pipeline

22
00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:55.969
encourages you to do more within the engine.

23
00:00:55.969 --> 00:00:57.490
And as we add more and more tools,

24
00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:58.670
you're gonna realize you're gonna be able to do

25
00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.509
just about everything within Unreal Engine.

26
00:01:01.509 --> 00:01:02.990
Now, currently you have the control rig

27
00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:06.390
and procedural rigging, which is a newer tool set,

28
00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.790
where you can build and animate controls

29
00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:09.950
either manually or using procedural rigging,

30
00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:11.549
as it says, as a template.

31
00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:13.069
That means you can actually use that

32
00:01:13.069 --> 00:01:15.230
for multiple types of characters,

33
00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:18.829
different sizes, different bones, or the number of bones.

34
00:01:18.829 --> 00:01:19.870
It's a really great tool,

35
00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:22.790
but this is a new set of tool sets that have been added.

36
00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:24.310
Control rig has been around for a little while,

37
00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:27.140
but it's improving constantly.

38
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.060
Now we also have the sequencer key frame,

39
00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:30.299
where everything in the control rig

40
00:01:30.299 --> 00:01:33.340
is animated within the level sequence.

41
00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.260
Now you'd build an empty level, as it states,

42
00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:36.459
and you would work that area

43
00:01:36.459 --> 00:01:38.780
and animate it directly within that scene,

44
00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.180
all in live, in real time.

45
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.659
So you'd be able to see what's going on

46
00:01:41.659 --> 00:01:42.819
as you're animating.

47
00:01:42.819 --> 00:01:45.099
You don't have to render it out first.

48
00:01:45.099 --> 00:01:47.739
And there are some newer tools I did wanna mention

49
00:01:47.739 --> 00:01:51.159
that currently they are within experimental stage.

50
00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:54.019
One of them is the skeleton mesh editing tool.

51
00:01:54.019 --> 00:01:56.219
Now this is available via a plugin, as it states.

52
00:01:56.219 --> 00:01:58.939
It is experimental, so it's not production ready.

53
00:01:58.939 --> 00:02:00.379
You'd suggest not using it.

54
00:02:00.379 --> 00:02:03.260
However, here is a link that is included

55
00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:07.019
for that US skeleton editing.

56
00:02:07.019 --> 00:02:09.060
Now here is the newer pipeline.

57
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.539
As you can see, there's some different coloring,

58
00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:13.500
which means it's traditionally done outside of Unreal

59
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.340
and moving more as you see the colors

60
00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:16.620
are becoming darker.

61
00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:20.819
That means it's even more done so within the engine.

62
00:02:20.819 --> 00:02:24.860
So this is a pretty typical new studio pipeline,

63
00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.780
utilizing everything within Unreal Engine.
